body:
@zxqfox You can do that pretty easily, either with multiple `<script type="module" src="...">` tags or with:

``` js
<script type="module">
import "https://example.com/module1.js";
import "https://example.com/module2.js";
import "https://example.com/module3.js";
</script>
```

This should be pretty rare though, as the number of top-level modules in an application is usually 1, maybe 2. In practice it's more likely that you will have a dependency tree of lots of non-top-level modules fanning out from one or two top-level modules via statements like `import $ from "https://example.com/jquery.js"` or `import { doStuff } from "./stuff-doer.js"`.

@meandmycode this thread is meant as a pull request review, preferably by implementers, of script type="module". The ways in which we arrived at that (instead of e.g. "use module") have already been discussed in other forums. Similarly, the fact that there are separate legacy/classic scripts like jQuery which you cannot import as a module was a decision made years ago. I'd prefer if you took such questions to another forum, e.g. es-discuss@mozilla.org or StackOverflow, and left this thread for collaboration between those speccing and implementing this feature. Maybe that wasn't clear from the OP. In any case, thanks to @annevk for sticking with that plan :)

@jakearchibald 

> I'm thinking about non-`<script>` contexts like workers.

The plan is to investigate allowing ways for workers to load module graphs, e.g. `new Worker("entry.js", { module: true })` (just a strawman). That way we are equipped to load entire module graphs, and allow import and export statements, and the like.

NEW QUESTION:

Consider

``` js
<script type="module">
import * as a1 from "./a.js";
window.a1 = a1;
</script>

<script type="module" crossorigin="anonymous">
import * as a2 from "./a.js";
window.a2 = a2;
</script>

<script type="module" crossorigin="use-credentials">
import * as a3 from "./a.js";
window.a3 = a3;
</script>

<script type="module">
console.log(window.a1 === window.a2);
console.log(window.a2 === window.a3);
</script>
```

What should this log? (I am here assuming default-defer so we can depend on execution order; if assuming default-async, then modify this to include a few promise contortions.)

That is, should the module memoization be done by URL only, or by (URL, credentials mode) pairs??

Hmm should we remove the fragment part of the URL when using it as a key? That is, what should this do:

``` html
<script type="module">
import * as a1 from "./a.js#foo";
import * as a2 from "./a.js#bar";
console.log(a1 === a2);
```

Should this do two fetches or one? Should it generate two module records or one?

For query, it seems clear that it needs to be two---the server could respond with totally different contents. But for hash, the server never sees the hash, so it seems like maybe we should strip it.
